        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Taking the name literally</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                BulletProof is a stain-resistant impregnating sealer, not armor. It does nothing against acid etching, and the maker suggests reapplying annually for best results. Wipe up spills promptly, clean with a pH-neutral product, and expect the busiest areas to need resealing before the rest.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting it dry on the surface</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The label says not to let the sealer dry on the surface, and LATICRETE's own technical note blames most haze on excess left to dry. Keep the stone wet for 10 to 15 minutes, then wipe it completely dry, rotating to a fresh towel every few square feet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Over-applying on dense polished stone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                BulletProof is positioned for porous stone and grout. Dense polished granite or tight marble absorbs little, so most of what you pour on stays on top and turns to haze. Water-test first. If the stone barely absorbs, use a light coat or skip sealing it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Missing the second-coat window</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If the stone needs a second coat, the data sheet says to apply it within 30 to 40 minutes of the first. Wait much longer and you are applying over a partly cured first coat, which raises the risk of residue.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging it before the full cure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Light use resumes after 6 to 8 hours, but full cure takes 24 to 72 hours. A stain test the next morning tells you little. Give it three days, then water-test the busiest areas and touch up any spot that still darkens.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who makes StoneTech BulletProof sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                LATICRETE International of Bethany, Connecticut. DuPont acquired StoneTech Professional in 2005, and LATICRETE agreed to buy DuPont's Surface Care business, including StoneTech, in 2014. Older bottles and reviews therefore often say DuPont StoneTech. The product sold today is a LATICRETE product with a current data sheet on its website.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is StoneTech BulletProof water-based?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. LATICRETE describes it as a water-based penetrating sealer, now designed without intentionally added PFAS. It goes on milky white and dries clear, cleans up with water, and the data sheet rates it low odor and non-flammable. That makes it a good fit for bathrooms and occupied homes where solvent fumes would be a problem.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does StoneTech BulletProof last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The data sheet gives expected wear of up to five years on interior surfaces and up to three years outdoors, but recommends reapplying annually for best results. In practice, water-test the busiest areas twice a year and reseal whatever absorbs. Kitchen counters near the sink usually need it first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is BulletProof sealer food safe?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                LATICRETE lists kitchen countertops among its uses, and the cured sealer sits inside the stone rather than on the surface. The data sheet we read does not cite a specific food-contact standard, so check the current label or ask LATICRETE if that matters to you, and keep the counter clear until the full cure.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use StoneTech BulletProof on marble floors and showers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Floors and walls are listed uses, marble and grout are listed substrates, it is rated for interior and exterior use, and a major tile retailer lists it as suitable for steam showers. Let new grout cure at least 72 hours first, and see our marble floor tile guide for traffic lanes and grout lines.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">StoneTech BulletProof vs 511 Impregnator: which is better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither is better everywhere. BulletProof is water-based, low odor and non-flammable, which suits bathrooms and occupied homes. 511 Impregnator is solvent-based, returns to light use sooner and is widely stocked, but needs strong ventilation. Neither stops etching. Choose by room, ventilation and how porous the stone is.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my stone look hazy after using BulletProof?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Excess sealer dried on the surface. LATICRETE says to rewet the area with sealer, agitate it with a white nylon pad and wipe it dry. For residue older than 24 hours, its technical note recommends STONETECH KlenzAll cleaner diluted with water, followed by resealing. Rotate towels often to avoid it next time.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

